    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.5.06-01",
      "block_type": "definition",
      "heading": "Cliffs, platforms, caves and residual landforms",
      "text": "Rocky coasts change through erosion, weathering and gravity. Their form depends on rock resistance, geological structure, wave exposure, water level and the history of change. Cliffs and platforms can develop as the shoreline retreats. Headlands and bays reflect uneven coastal resistance and other controls. Caves, arches, stacks and stumps can form through progressive erosion of a headland, but the full sequence is a model rather than a compulsory future for every rock projection. Read the resource’s weaknesses, layers and viewing direction before explaining its landforms.",
