    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.5.06-02",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A sea cliff is a steep coastal slope or face. Waves can erode its base, while weathering weakens material and gravity removes unsupported sections. A wave-cut notch is an indentation produced near the cliff base where erosion is concentrated within the relevant water-level zone. Repeated hydraulic action and abrasion can deepen the notch. As support is removed, the overlying material may fail, adding debris at the foot. Removal of that debris can expose the new base to further erosion. Retreat results from repeated linked actions, not the entire cliff sliding steadily backward as a single intact block.",
