    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.5.06-03",
      "block_type": "worked_application",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "The shape and stability of a cliff also depend on bedding, joints and groundwater. A massive resistant rock can produce a different failure style from layered material with weaker bands. Water within a slope can affect strength and pressure, helping certain failures under appropriate conditions. A steep face alone does not establish the exact mechanism of collapse. In a resource specifically showing an undercut base, overhang and fallen blocks, the support-loss sequence is justified. A resource showing a curved slump surface needs an explanation suited to that structure rather than a rockfall story copied from another example.",
