    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.5.06-05",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "As a cliff retreats, a relatively gently sloping rock surface may remain in front of it: a wave-cut platform. Erosion and removal work on the exposed rock while the cliff line moves landward. Parts of the platform may be covered by water at higher tides and exposed at lower tides. It is not necessarily a sandy beach, though loose sediment can cover parts of it. The rock surface, cliff relationship and tidal exposure provide evidence for identification. A flat strip of sand without rock or retreat information should not automatically be called a wave-cut platform.",
