    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.5.06-07",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Headlands are projections of land into the sea, while bays are coastal indentations between or beside them. In a simple differential-erosion model, less resistant rock is removed more rapidly, creating bays, while more resistant rock remains as headlands. The model is clearest on a discordant coastline, where bands of more and less resistant rock run at right angles to the coast and hydraulic action and abrasion wear back the less resistant bands faster. The actual pattern also depends on structure and exposure. A bay may have a depositional beach within it, so its existence does not imply that every part is undergoing rapid erosion at the time observed.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-1.5.06-1",
        "CL-6137-1.5.06-2",
