    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.5.06-08",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Wave refraction can help modify the pattern. As waves enter water of different depths, parts of the wave slow at different times and the wave fronts bend. In some settings this concentrates wave energy towards exposed headlands and spreads it within bays. The process can reinforce contrasts in erosion and deposition, but the exact result depends on bathymetry and wave approach. A simple plan-view diagram needs consistent wave-front and depth geometry. Arrows pointing towards a headland are not a substitute for showing or explaining why the wave pattern changes.",
