    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.5.06-09",
      "block_type": "worked_application",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A resource might identify rock A as relatively resistant, rock B as less resistant and an initially straighter coast crossing both. Later, B is set back into an indentation while A projects seaward. The paired change supports the differential-resistance explanation. Do not describe the headland as being built outward by erosion: it may project because adjacent material was removed faster. Relative projection does not necessarily mean the headland itself has not retreated. Both rock types can erode while maintaining a contrast in coastline position.",
