    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.5.06-10",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A sea cave can develop where waves enlarge a weakness in a cliff or headland. Hydraulic action and abrasion work into joints, faults or weaker rock, gradually extending an opening. A cave has an entrance and an enclosed or partially enclosed chamber that does not yet form an open passage through the entire headland. Describe the opening and the surrounding rock before explaining its growth. A dark patch in a photograph might be shadow rather than a cave, so interpretation needs depth cues, a clear entrance or supporting information.",
      "claim_ids": [
