    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.5.06-11",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "If erosion extends a cave through a narrow headland, or caves from opposite sides join, an arch can form. The arch has an opening passing through the rock with a roof remaining above it. The route through distinguishes it from a blind cave. The roof’s survival depends on the material and structure as well as the size of the opening. Weathering, erosion and gravity can weaken it over time. A diagram of an arch should show a continuous connection between the remaining rock on either side rather than an isolated pillar mistakenly labelled as a roof.",
      "claim_ids": [
