    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.5.06-14",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "The cave–arch–stack–stump model combines enlargement, breakthrough, collapse and reduction. A useful description preserves the order and the defining spatial change at each stage. Caves may also collapse without producing a long-lived arch, and geological structure can produce other residual forms. The model should therefore be applied to the evidence shown rather than treated as a prediction that every headland must display all stages simultaneously. The same coast can include features of different ages and different histories without fitting a neat left-to-right sequence in a photograph.",
      "claim_ids": [
