    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.5.06-16",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Viewing direction is central to identification. A side photograph of a headland may hide whether an opening passes right through; a plan view can reveal connection and separation but not easily establish height. A cross-section can show the notch and platform relationship while omitting alongshore variation. Combine the views where available. If only one view is supplied and it cannot resolve a distinction, state the most supported interpretation and the missing evidence rather than inventing an unseen opening or a roof collapse that was not recorded.",
