    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.5.06-18",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "These erosional landforms connect to beach sediment budgets because removed cliff material can supply fragments to the shore. Their size, composition and subsequent transport depend on the rock and wave conditions. A seawall can reduce direct cliff-base attack locally but may alter sediment supply or nearby processes; the physical sequence helps explain that management trade-off. Likewise, falling rock and changing tides create reasons to observe from safe approved positions or use supplied images. Understanding the landform does not require approaching an unstable cliff or entering a sea cave.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-1.5.06-1",
