      "block_id": "BL-6137-1.5.06-21",
      "block_type": "worked_application",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A second original plan can place a cave on one side of a headland and an opening visible from the opposite side. If a surveyed passage connects them beneath a roof, an arch interpretation is supported. Without the passage evidence, the two openings could remain separate caves. This is a useful test because it identifies what extra information resolves the distinction. It also shows why a ground-level photograph from one side can be insufficient even when the most familiar textbook sequence suggests a later arch.",
